<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=aircraft-carrier">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aircraft carrier</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as fleet carriers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An aircraft carrier is a warship designed to carry, launch and recover aircraft, serving as a mobile air base at sea. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> built one of the world's most capable carrier fleets in the interwar years and used it in the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> in 1941. The loss of four fleet carriers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-midway">Battle of Midway</a> in 1942 was a turning point in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s war in the Pacific.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aircraft-carrier.jpg" alt="Aircraft carrier" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Airman Robert Baker, U.S. Navy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USS_John_C._Stennis_(CVN-74)_and_HMS_Illustrious_(R_06)_in_the_Persian_Gulf_on_April_9,_1998.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The aircraft carrier followed the first flight of the Wright brothers at Kitty Hawk in 1903. On 14 November 1910 Eugene Burton Ely made the first experimental take-off of a Curtiss Pusher airplane from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> ship, the cruiser USS Birmingham. On 9 May 1912 Commander Charles Samson of the Royal Naval Air Service made the first take-off from a ship under way, flying from the battleship HMS Hibernia, the first practical demonstration of the carrier at sea.</p><p>Early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Japanese ship Wakamiya conducted the first carrier-launched air raid, on 6 September 1914, lowering Farman seaplanes into the water by crane to attack ships off Qingdao. Neither target was hit. The first airstrike by aircraft flown off a carrier was the Tondern raid in July 1918, when seven Sopwith Camels launched from HMS Furious damaged a German airbase and destroyed two zeppelins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An aircraft carrier is a warship with a full-length flight deck and hangar that make it a mobile airbase at sea. It lets a naval force project air power far from national territory without relying on local airfields. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the carrier has replaced the battleship as the flagship of a fleet, and it has largely changed naval battle from gunfire to air strikes beyond visual range.</p><p>Fleet carriers operate with the main fleet and are the largest and fastest. Escort carriers, smaller and slower, defended convoys and were often built on merchant hulls. Light carriers were fast enough for the main fleet but smaller. Aircraft are launched by catapult (CATOBAR), which suits heavy jets, or by short take-off and vertical landing (STOVL), which is cheaper to operate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Only about 20 aircraft can be on the flight deck at once, and most are kept in a hangar several decks below and raised by elevator. Munitions are stored low, usually below the waterline so the area can be flooded in an emergency.</p><p>Carriers have been called the largest weapon system ever created. Admiral Sir Mark Stanhope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> said that countries that aspire to strategic international influence have aircraft carriers, and Henry Kissinger called one 100,000 tons of diplomacy. A few have been kept as museum ships, among them USS Yorktown, USS Intrepid and USS Midway.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aircraft carrier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Battle of Midway in June 1942, which cost Japan four <b>fleet carriers</b>, and the Guadalcanal campaign of August 1942 to February 1943 surrendered the strategic initiative to the United States for good.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan's loss of four <b>fleet carriers</b> ended its naval superiority in the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-7">7 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1918</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Eugene Burton Ely · Charles Samson · Edwin Harris Dunning · <a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Aircraft left ships earlier: Eugene Burton Ely took off from the cruiser USS Birmingham on 14 November 1910, and the Japanese ship Wakamiya launched seaplanes in 1914. HMS Argus in 1918 is the first ship called capable of both launching and recovering aircraft.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/hot-air-balloon">Hot-air balloon<span>1783</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">Aeroplane<span>1903</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=aircraft-carrier">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
